<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Awan dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">2400 BCE – 2100 BCE (approx.) · capital Awan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first known royal house of Elam, which fought and treated with the kings of Akkad and whose last king briefly ruled Susa and the Diyala region.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Awan was a highland district of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, probably north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dezful">Dezful</a> or in Luristan, whose exact site is unknown. An Elamite king list from Susa names twelve kings of Awan, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> says kingship passed briefly to Awan, reflecting its power in the 24th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings of Awan led a coalition of Elamite lands and resisted the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> record victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and its allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> later concluded a treaty, written in Elamite, with a ruler of Awan, probably Hita. The gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humban">Humban</a> were central to Elamite religion. The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutik-inshushinak">Puzur-Inshushinak</a>, built at Susa and left inscriptions in Akkadian and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a>, a script largely deciphered only in 2020.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad's</a> collapse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutik-inshushinak">Puzur-Inshushinak</a> expanded as far as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, but he was driven back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> around 2100 BCE. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, and leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki dynasty</a> and the wider <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/awan-dynasty.jpg" alt="Awan dynasty" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Near_East_2600BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2400 BCE – 2100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Awan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hita, Puzur-Inshushinak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Elamite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elamite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Awan, Awan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2250&amp;focus=awan-dynasty" aria-label="Awan dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5915 963 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Awan dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6235 1219L6241 1263M6235 1219L6129 1232"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6241 1263h0M6129 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6235 1219h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6235 1219h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Awan. <a href="/atlas?year=-2250&amp;focus=awan-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

